From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Subject: crashpad: ppc64: map kCPUArchitecturePPC64 in MinidumpSystemInfoWriter

The Raptor Engineering ppc64 crashpad port carried in the ppc64le series
(crashpad/0001-Implement-support-for-PPC64-on-Linux.patch) appends
kCPUArchitecturePPC64 to crashpad::CPUArchitecture and returns it from
SystemSnapshotLinux::GetCPUArchitecture(), but never teaches the minidump
writer about it. minidump/minidump_system_info_writer.cc maps that enum onto
the MINIDUMP_SYSTEM_INFO ProcessorArchitecture field with a switch whose
default is NOTREACHED(), so on ppc64le the first thing the handler does when
it writes a report is trap:

  MinidumpCPUArchitecture cpu_architecture;
  switch (system_snapshot->GetCPUArchitecture()) {
    case kCPUArchitectureX86:     ... kMinidumpCPUArchitectureX86
    case kCPUArchitectureX86_64:  ... kMinidumpCPUArchitectureAMD64
    case kCPUArchitectureARM:     ... kMinidumpCPUArchitectureARM
    case kCPUArchitectureARM64:   ... kMinidumpCPUArchitectureARM64
    case kCPUArchitectureRISCV64: ... kMinidumpCPUArchitectureRISCV64Breakpad
    default:                          NOTREACHED();
  }

NOTREACHED() is base::ImmediateCrash(), which on powerpc64 is a `trap`
instruction, so the handler dies with SIGTRAP/TRAP_BRKPT rather than writing
the dump. Observed as pairs of chrome_crashpad_handler coredumps five seconds
apart -- the handler traps, the --monitor-self handler traps the same way
snapshotting it -- with 0-byte files left in the database's new/ directory and
nothing ever reaching pending/ or completed/. Because both handlers are then
gone, the browser or renderer crash that asked for the dump falls through to
the kernel core pattern instead, which is why the only usable evidence on
ppc64le has been systemd-coredump's unsymbolized stacks.

Verified in a core from a trapped handler: r3 (the GetCPUArchitecture() return
value, unmodified between the compare chain and the trap) held 8, which is
kCPUArchitecturePPC64's value once it is appended after kCPUArchitectureRISCV64,
and the MINIDUMP_SYSTEM_INFO at the writer's +128 was still entirely zero with
ProcessorArchitecture at its kMinidumpCPUArchitectureUnknown (0xffff) default,
i.e. the trap fired before any field was stored.

No new constant is needed: minidump/minidump_extensions.h already defines
kMinidumpCPUArchitecturePPC64 = 0x8002, the value Breakpad used for ppc64
(MD_CPU_ARCHITECTURE_PPC64), so dumps written with it stay readable by existing
tooling. The new case is inserted ahead of the x86 case rather than after the
RISCV64 one purely so the hunk's context stays stable across crashpad rolls;
switch case order is immaterial.

This is the only architecture-enumerating site in crashpad's dump-*writing*
path that the port misses. The two reader-side sites,
snapshot/minidump/minidump_context_converter.cc and
snapshot/minidump/system_snapshot_minidump.cc, fall back to
kCPUArchitectureUnknown instead of trapping, so they degrade rather than crash
and are left alone here.

--- a/third_party/crashpad/crashpad/minidump/minidump_system_info_writer.cc
+++ b/third_party/crashpad/crashpad/minidump/minidump_system_info_writer.cc
@@ -119,6 +119,9 @@
 
   MinidumpCPUArchitecture cpu_architecture;
   switch (system_snapshot->GetCPUArchitecture()) {
+    case kCPUArchitecturePPC64:
+      cpu_architecture = kMinidumpCPUArchitecturePPC64;
+      break;
     case kCPUArchitectureX86:
       cpu_architecture = kMinidumpCPUArchitectureX86;
       break;
